+---
+status: design-intent
+---
+
+# todo-check Validates a Citation's Line Number — Implementation Plan
+
+**Goal:** `scripts/todo-check.js` reports `past end` and exits 1 when a TODO.md
+entry cites a `path:N` or `path:N-M` line range past the end of the file it
+names, instead of silently exiting 0 as it does today.
+
+**Architecture:** Reuse `docs-check.js`'s own line counter rather than writing
+a second one. `todo-check.js` reads a citation's line number from the two
+places it can appear — a backtick-quoted `PATHISH` span, and the target of a
+markdown link — and checks each against `lineCount`. Fixing the existing
+`linksIn` so a `:N` suffix is stripped before the file-existence check is part
+of the same change: today that suffix makes a real file read as a dead one.
+
+**Tech Stack:** Node's built-in `node:test`, no new dependency — `package.json`
+declares none beyond the runtime itself (`"test": "node --test"`, version
+0.79.0).
+
+**Spec:** [docs/plans/2026-09-25-todo-line-and-multiplier-design.md](2026-09-25-todo-line-and-multiplier-design.md)
+§1. That design covers three unrelated items in one document (§1 todo-check,
+§2 a Sonnet-multiplier measurement, §3 TODO.md housekeeping); this plan
+delivers only §1 and the todo-check half of §3's first bullet. The rest is
+struck in the Coverage table below with the task that actually owns it.
+
+## Global Constraints
+
+From `node scripts/map.js`:
+- `lib/` is the logic, as functions tested directly; `scripts/` are thin
+  wrappers over `lib/` — but `todo-check.js` and `docs-check.js` are already
+  filed only under `scripts/`, with no `lib/` counterpart, so keeping the new
+  code there matches the existing shape rather than introducing one.
+- `tests/` is `node --test`, one file per module or behaviour — the three new
+  fixtures go into the existing `tests/todo-check.test.js`, not a new file.
+- `docs/plans` is filed with role `plan` — this document belongs there, and
+  the design it argues from is filed the same way.
+- No `.fankeel/map.md` conflict: nothing here contradicts a page the map
+  lists as current, and nothing here is `design-intent` being treated as
+  built.
+
+From `package.json`: `"test": "node --test"`; no dependency list to respect
+beyond Node's own built-ins.
+
+No `CLAUDE.md` exists in this repository (checked at the repository root) —
+no additional conventions to copy from it.
+
+## Task 1: todo-check validates a citation's line number
+
+**Files:**
+- Modify: `scripts/docs-check.js` — export `lineCount`
+- Modify: `scripts/todo-check.js` — read a citation's line number from a
+  backtick span or a link target, check it against `lineCount`, report
+  `past end`; fix `linksIn` to strip `:N`/`:N-M` before the existence check
+- Modify: `TODO.md` — bump the "enforces all nine" count to ten with the new
+  clause, and remove the delivered `〔docs〕todo-check 不驗行號` bullet
+- Test: `tests/todo-check.test.js` — three new fixtures
+
+**Interfaces:**
+- Consumes: none — this plan has only one task
+- Produces: none — nothing later depends on this
+
+**Dispatch:** implementer, sonnet
+
+### 1. `scripts/docs-check.js` — export `lineCount`
+
+`lineCount` is already implemented at `scripts/docs-check.js:155`; only its
+export is missing. In `scripts/docs-check.js`, replace the `module.exports`
+line (currently the file's last line) with:
+
+```js
+module.exports = { scan, report, parseArgs, resolveRef, LINK, CODE, PATHISH, external, readFile, isMarkdown, lineCount };
+```
+
+### 2. `scripts/todo-check.js` — import `PATHISH` and `lineCount`
+
+In `scripts/todo-check.js`, after the existing `const docs = require('../lib/docs.js');`
+line, add:
+
+```js
+const { PATHISH, lineCount } = require('./docs-check.js');
+```
+
+### 3. `scripts/todo-check.js` — a `BACKTICK` regex beside `LINK`/`EXTERNAL`
+
+In `scripts/todo-check.js`, immediately after the existing `const EXTERNAL = /^(?:[a-z][a-z0-9+.-]*:|#|\/\/)/i;`
+line, add:
+
+```js
+// A backtick span is not necessarily a citation — most are code fragments
+// with no line number at all. Only one shaped like `PATHISH` with a `:N`
+// counts, which is what the past-end check below tests for.
+const BACKTICK = /`([^`]+)`/g;
+```
+
+### 4. `scripts/todo-check.js` — replace `linksIn` with `linkTargets` and `pastEnd`
+
+The whole point is a link's line number, so `linksIn` has to carry it. In
+`scripts/todo-check.js`, replace this whole function:
+
+```js
+function linksIn(text) {
+    const out = [];
+    LINK.lastIndex = 0;
+    let m;
+    while ((m = LINK.exec(text)) !== null) {
+        const target = m[1].trim().split(/\s+/)[0].replace(/^<|>$/g, '');
+        if (!target || EXTERNAL.test(target)) continue;
+        out.push(target.split('#')[0]);
+    }
+    return out;
+}
+```
+
+with these three, still in `scripts/todo-check.js`:
+
+```js
+// Every link target, split into the path and the `:N`/`:N-M` it names — the
+// two forms `docs-check.js`'s own `PATHISH` already reads. A target with no
+// `:N` gets `from`/`to` of `null`, which is how the past-end check below
+// knows there is no line to check. Stripping the `:N` here, before the
+// existence check that reads `.path`, is the fix: today a link written
+// `path:99999` is read whole and reported `dead link` for the wrong reason —
+// the file exists, only the line does not.
+function linkTargets(text) {
+    const out = [];
+    LINK.lastIndex = 0;
+    let m;
+    while ((m = LINK.exec(text)) !== null) {
+        const target = m[1].trim().split(/\s+/)[0].replace(/^<|>$/g, '');
+        if (!target || EXTERNAL.test(target)) continue;
+        const stripped = target.split('#')[0];
+        const span = PATHISH.exec(stripped);
+        out.push(span
+            ? { path: span[1], from: span[2] ? Number(span[2]) : null, to: span[3] ? Number(span[3]) : (span[2] ? Number(span[2]) : null) }
+            : { path: stripped, from: null, to: null });
+    }
+    return out;
+}
+
+// The path alone, for the two callers that never needed the line number: the
+// dead-link/stale-citation loop below, and the "linksIn keeps only
+// in-repository targets" contract the existing test already asserts.
+function linksIn(text) {
+    return linkTargets(text).map((l) => l.path);
+}
+
+// A citation is wrong when the file it names exists but the line does not.
+// `total` is `null` for a file that does not exist, and that is `dead link`'s
+// question, not this one's — a citation with no line to check (`from` was
+// never reached, or `total` is `null`) is silently fine here. Role is not
+// asked: `past end` does not care whether the file is reference or report.
+function pastEnd(base, p, from, to) {
+    const total = lineCount(base, p);
+    if (total === null || (from <= total && to <= total)) return null;
+    return p + ':' + (from === to ? from : from + '-' + to) + ' is past end — ' + p + ' has ' + total + ' lines.';
+}
+```
+
+### 5. `scripts/todo-check.js` — wire the check into `check()`
+
+In `scripts/todo-check.js`, in `check()`, replace this loop:
+
+```js
+        for (const target of linksIn(entry.text)) {
+            const full = path.resolve(base, target);
+            if (!fs.existsSync(full)) {
+                problems.push({
+                    line: entry.line,
+                    kind: 'dead link',
+                    detail: target + ' does not exist. Either the entry is finished and should be removed, or the detail moved.',
+                });
+                continue;
+            }
+            const role = docs.roleOf(tree, path.relative(base, full));
+            if (STALE_ROLES.includes(role)) {
+                problems.push({
+                    line: entry.line,
+                    kind: 'stale citation',
+                    detail: target + ' is filed as ' + role + ', a role that records a moment rather than the present. Point at the code this is about, or at a reference page.',
+                });
+            }
+        }
+    }
+```
+
+with this, still in `scripts/todo-check.js`:
+
+```js
+        for (const link of linkTargets(entry.text)) {
+            const full = path.resolve(base, link.path);
+            if (!fs.existsSync(full)) {
+                problems.push({
+                    line: entry.line,
+                    kind: 'dead link',
+                    detail: link.path + ' does not exist. Either the entry is finished and should be removed, or the detail moved.',
+                });
+                continue;
+            }
+            if (link.from !== null) {
+                const detail = pastEnd(base, link.path, link.from, link.to);
+                if (detail) problems.push({ line: entry.line, kind: 'past end', detail });
+            }
+            const role = docs.roleOf(tree, path.relative(base, full));
+            if (STALE_ROLES.includes(role)) {
+                problems.push({
+                    line: entry.line,
+                    kind: 'stale citation',
+                    detail: link.path + ' is filed as ' + role + ', a role that records a moment rather than the present. Point at the code this is about, or at a reference page.',
+                });
+            }
+        }
+        // A backtick span carries no existence claim — only a link does — but
+        // one shaped `path:N` still claims a line, and that claim is checked
+        // regardless of whether it is also linked anywhere in the entry.
+        BACKTICK.lastIndex = 0;
+        let bt;
+        while ((bt = BACKTICK.exec(entry.text)) !== null) {
+            const span = PATHISH.exec(bt[1].trim());
+            if (!span || !span[2]) continue;
+            const from = Number(span[2]);
+            const to = span[3] ? Number(span[3]) : from;
+            const detail = pastEnd(base, span[1], from, to);
+            if (detail) problems.push({ line: entry.line, kind: 'past end', detail });
+        }
+    }
+```
+
+`past end` lands between `dead link` and `stale citation` for the same link
+target, and `problems.sort((a, b) => a.line - b.line)` a few lines below is a
+stable sort, so that insertion order is what a reader sees for one entry.
+
